 a Grade II listed building.","wpu":"St James' Church, Dover","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/St_James_Street%2C_Dover_-_geograph.org.uk_-_822238.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Castle House","g":"sights","k":"guest_house","la":51.126537,"lo":1.317822,"d":1071,"w":"https://castle-guesthouse.co.uk/","ph":"+44 1304 201656","wifi":1},{"n":"Drop Redoubt","g":"sights","k":"fort","la":51.122802,"lo":1.307675,"d":1075,"w":"https://doverwesternheights.org/drop-redoubt/"},{"n":"Canon's Gate House","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":51.126604,"lo":1.321782,"d":1075},{"n":"Secret Wartime Tunnels","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":51.126477,"lo":1.32328,"d":1078},{"n":"The Underground Hopital","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":51.12671,"lo":1.322456,"d":1093},{"n":"Roman Painted House","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":51.125457,"lo":1.311963,"d":1094},{"n":"Number One Guest House","g":"sights","k":"guest_house","la":51.126794,"lo":1.316743,"d":1112,"w":"https://number1guesthouse.co.uk","wifi":1},{"n":"Knights Templar Church","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":51.119051,"lo":1.30352,"d":1172,"w":"https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/knights-templar-church-dover/"},{"n":"Shakespeare's Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":51.113041,"lo":1.304328,"d":1179},{"n":"White Cliffs of Dover","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":51.127319,"lo":1.326957,"d":1245,"ds":"The White Cliffs of Dover are the region of English coastline facing the Strait of Dover and France. The cliff face, which reaches a height of 350 feet (110 m), owes its striking appearance to its composition of chalk accented by streaks of black flint, deposited during the Late Cretaceous. The cliffs, on both sides of the town of Dover in Kent, stretch for eight miles (13 km).","wpu":"White Cliffs of Dover","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/960px-White_Cliffs_of_Dove