"img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/The_River_Dour_near_its_source%2C_Alkham_Valley_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1164512.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Archcliffe Fort","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.115,"lo":1.307222,"d":919,"ds":"Archcliffe Fort is a former military installation in Dover, England. It is situated at the base of the Dover Western Heights and overlooks the approaches to the port of Dover. A watchtower was erected on the site in 1370 but this was replaced with a more substantial fort by Henry VIII by 1539.","wpu":"Archcliffe Fort","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Archcliffe_Fort%2C_Dover_%28geograph_4607184%29.jpg/960px-Archcliffe_Fort%2C_Dover_%28geograph_4607184%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Edmund's Chapel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.1274,"lo":1.3093,"d":1376,"ds":"St Edmund's Chapel is a church in Dover, England, dedicated to St Edmund. It was completed in 1262 as a wayside chapel or chapel of rest for the cemetery for the poor beside the Maison Dieu, just outside the enclosed part of the medieval town, a short distance above Biggin Gate, and for pilgrims setting off for Thomas Becket's shrine at Canterbury Cathedral.","wpu":"St Edmund's Chapel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/St._Edmund%27s_Chapel_in_Dover.jpg/960px-St._Edmund%27s_Chapel_in_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Burgoyne","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.136389,"lo":1.321111,"d":2157,"ds":"Fort Burgoyne, originally known as Castle Hill Fort, was built in the 1860s as one of the Palmerston forts around Dover in southeast England. It was built to a polygonal system with detached eastern and western redoubts, to guard the high ground northeast of the strategic port of Dover, just north of Dover Castle.","wpu":"Fort Burgoyne","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Fort_Burgoyne%2C_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"High Meadow","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.129,"lo":1.289,"d":2541,"ds":"High Meadow is a 21.3-hectare (53-acre) Local Nature Reserve (LNR) on the western outskirts of Dover in Kent. It is owned by Dover Town Council and managed by The White Cliffs Countryside Partnership. It was declared a Local Nature Reserve in 2006.","wpu":"High Meadow","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/High_Meadow_12.jpg/960px-High_Meadow_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Langdon Bay (Kent)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.1336,"lo":1.351,"d":2843,"ds":"Langdon Bay is a bay in east Kent, England. It is two miles east of the town of Dover, and is named after the nearby villages of Langdon, East Langdon and West Langdon. The cliffs around it are known as the Langdon Cliffs.","wpu":"Langdon Bay (Kent)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_White_Cliffs_of_Dover.jpg/960px-The_White_Cliffs_of_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Samphire Hoe","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.1056,"lo":1.275,"d":3387,"ds":"Samphire Hoe is a country park situated 2 miles (3 km) west of Dover in Kent in southeast England. The park was created by using 4.9 million cubic metres of chalk marl from the Channel Tunnel excavations and is located at the bottom of a section of the White Cliffs of Dover. The site is owned by Getlink, and managed by the White Cliffs Countryside Project.","wpu":"Samphire Hoe","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Samphire_Hoe_-_geograph.org.uk_-_12768.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Fan Bay Deep Shelter","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.136333,"lo":1.360431,"d":3547,"ds":"Fan Bay Deep Shelter is a series of tunnels constructed during World War II as accommodation for Fan Bay Battery artillery battery, 23 metres down in the White Cliffs of Dover at Fan Bay near the Port of Dover. The tunnels and gun battery were built by the Royal Engineers between 20 November 1940 and 28 February 1941. The site is maintained by the National Trust.","wpu":"Fan Bay Deep Shelter","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Fan_Bay_Deep_Shelter_%28inside%29.jpg/960px-Fan_Bay_Deep_Shelter_%28inside%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Swingate Mill, Guston","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.150833,"lo":1.334583,"d":3897,"ds":"Swingate Mill is a Grade II listed tower mill in Guston, Kent, England that was built in 1849.","wpu":"Swingate Mill, Guston","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Guston_Windmill_1913.jpg/960px-Guston_Windmill_1913.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Park Hill","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.147,"lo":1.279,"d":4394,"ds":"Old Park Hill is a 40-hectare (100-acre) nature reserve north of Dover in Kent. It is managed by the Kent Wildlife Trust (KWT). It is in the Kent Downs Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.","wpu":"Old Park Hill","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Old_Park_Hill_8.jpg/960px-Old_Park_Hill_8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kearsney, Kent","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.148056,"lo":1.269167,"d":4950,"ds":"Kearsney is a village in Kent, England. At one time it would have been called a hamlet, there being no church there. The population of the village is included in the civil parish of Temple Ewell.","wpu":"Kearsney, Kent","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Street-Map-Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"St. Radegund's Abbey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.131944,"lo":1.250833,"d":5105,"ds":"St. Radegund's Abbey at Bradsole was a medieval monastic house in the parish of Hougham Without near Dover in southeast England. It was dedicated to Radegund, the sixth-century Merovingian princess, who, once married to the unsavory King Chlothar I, turned to a life of asceticism and charitable works.","wpu":"St. Radegund's Abbey","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/St_Radigunds_Abbey_Gatehouse_-_geograph.org.uk_-_941783.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Temple Ewell","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.1526,"lo":1.2695,"d":5299,"ds":"Temple Ewell is a civil parish and historic village in the county of Kent, England. The village is part of the Dover district of Kent, and forms part of the Dover urban area. It is situated three miles North West of the town of Dover.","wpu":"Temple Ewell","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Temple_Ewell_Parish_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Church Whitfield","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.1655,"lo":1.303,"d":5521,"ds":"Church Whitfield is a village in the civil parish of Whitfield, and just north of Dover, in Kent, England. Village population is included in Whitfield civil parish. Part of the village is called Pineham.","wpu":"Church Whitfield","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/St_Peter%27s_Church%2C_Church_Whitfield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"On 18 June 2000, just before midnight, 58 dead bodies were found in a lorry in the port town of Dover, United Kingdom. Two people were found alive but injured and taken to hospital.","wpu":"Dover lorry deaths","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Dover_Port.jpg/960px-Dover_Port.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}